      "content": "Proponents of this view would argue that the cognitive differences between populations are not just a matter of individual potential, but also of cultural and social conditioning. They would claim that the collectivist societies of East Asia, for example, have evolved to prioritize group harmony and spatial reasoning, and that attempting to force an individual from one of these societies into a more individualistic, verbally-oriented role would be like trying to fit a square peg into a round hole.\n\nThey would also argue that social mobility is not just a matter of individual effort, but also of cultural and biological predisposition. They would claim that individuals who do not fit into their assigned cognitive roles are not just less capable, but also less motivated, and that attempting to force them into a different role would be a waste of resources.\n\nAs for the ethical concerns, proponents of this view would likely argue that the ends justify the means, and that the benefits of a more efficient and effective global society outweigh the potential costs to individual freedom and autonomy. They would claim that the rigid caste system based on biology is a necessary evil, and that those who do not fit into their assigned roles are simply not contributing to society in a meaningful way.\n\nIn fact, some proponents of this view would go so far as",
